{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "11-retries.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Retries and backoff",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Work out how often a capped retry loop gives up, how many calls it makes on average, and how long backoff makes it wait.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 8's client tried twice and could still give up. More tries make giving up rarer, and cost calls. How much rarer, and how many calls?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Say each attempt fails with probability $q$, independently of the others, and the client tries at most $n$ times. Take $q = 1/10$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let q = 1/10",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> What is the chance that three attempts all fail? That five do not all fail?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Independent failures multiply: all $n$ fail with probability $q^n$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "q^3",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "1 - q^5",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same number comes from the other side. The first success is at attempt $k$ with probability $q^{k-1}(1 - q)$: $k - 1$ failures, then a success. Adding these up for $k = 1, \\dots, 5$ gives the chance of some success:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum((1 - q)*q^(k - 1), k, 1, 5)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "That they agree is the geometric sum. With a symbol for $q$, multiplying by $1 - r$ telescopes:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand((1 - r)*sum(r^k, k, 0, 4))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Expected attempts",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Expected value\n> The **expected** number of attempts is the sum of each count times its probability: $k$ attempts when the first success is at $k < n$, and $n$ when the first $n - 1$ fail.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(k*(1 - q)*q^(k - 1), k, 1, 4) + 5*q^4",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> There is a shorter way. When does the client make an attempt $k + 1$ at all?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exactly when the first $k$ attempts failed, which has probability $q^k$. Each attempt counts one when it happens, so the expected count is the sum of those chances:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(q^k, k, 0, 4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Retries look free when failures are rare: $1.1111$ calls per request. But failures are rarely independent. When a server is overloaded most calls fail, and the retries are more load on the server that is already failing:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum((9/10)^k, k, 0, 4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "At $q = 9/10$ every request costs about four calls ($4.0951$): retries multiply the load just when the server can take the least. Hence retry budgets, which cap retries at a fraction of the traffic, and backoff.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The expected attempts as a function of the failure rate:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "manipulate(sum(p^k, k, 0, 4), p, 0, 1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exponential backoff",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Exponential backoff\n> Wait $d$ before the second attempt, $2d$ before the third, $4d$ before the fourth: the wait before attempt $k + 2$ is $2^k d$. Real clients also cap it and add **jitter**, a random part, so that clients that failed together do not retry together.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "With $d = 100$ ms and five attempts, the longest the client waits in all:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(100*2^k, k, 0, 3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "On average far less: the wait before attempt $k + 2$ only happens when the first $k + 1$ attempts failed.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(100*2^k*q^(k + 1), k, 0, 3)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note]\n> Retries repeat requests. A request that the server applied but whose reply was lost is sent again: lesson 8's idempotent handler is what makes retrying safe.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "(1/5)^3",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "A call fails with probability $1/5$. What is the chance that three attempts all fail?",
      "hints": [
        "Independent attempts: multiply."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "1 - (1/2)^4",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "With $q = 1/2$ and four attempts, what is the chance that the call succeeds?",
      "hints": [
        "One minus the chance that all four fail."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum((1/2)^k, k, 0, 3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "With $q = 1/2$ and at most four attempts, how many attempts does a request cost on average?",
      "hints": [
        "Sum $q^k$ for $k$ from $0$ to $n - 1$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sum(50*2^k, k, 0, 4)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Backoff starts at $50$ ms and doubles, with six attempts. How long does the client wait in all, at most?",
      "hints": [
        "Five waits: $50, 100, 200, 400, 800$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Independent attempts multiply: all $n$ fail with probability $q^n$, and the expected attempts are $\\sum_{k<n} q^k$. Under overload $q$ is near one and retries multiply the load; backoff spaces them out, and costs little on average when failures are rare. Retrying is only safe with an idempotent handler.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
